<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!--  Chapter 3: Exploring Data                                         -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Data-first chapter. Deliberately light: notice patterns, ask      -->
<!--  questions, read a pivot table. No formal definitions of mean,     -->
<!--  variance, missingness taxonomy, etc. - those are introduced only  -->
<!--  when they are needed, in later chapters:                          -->
<!--  This chapter's job is only to make the reader curious enough to   -->
<!--  want those later chapters.                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-exploring-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exploring Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-exploring-data">
    <p>
      There is no formula to learn in this chapter, and nothing here is the
      <q>correct</q> way to analyse anything.
      The goal is smaller and more important than that: open the data, look at
      it, and let it surprise you.
      Every method introduced later in this book, such as cleaning,
      multidimensional visualisations, factors, formal treatment of missing
      values, and measures of variability, exists to answer a question that this
      chapter is going to raise and deliberately leave open.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 0, DRAFT A (ACTIVE): why explore at all - kenya crash data -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-malaria-map-hook">
    <title>Why Explore At All?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Since 2012, the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA) has
        recorded road crash data across Kenya, including information on county,
        road, time, vehicle type, road user category.
        This is published in summaries annually on their website.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-crashes">
      <title>Who is dying on Kenya's roads, and where?</title>
      <p>
        The NTSA publishes two kinds of records.
        The first are daily comparative reports, which summarise fatalities and
        injuries across six road user categories - pedestrians, passengers,
        drivers, pillion passengers, pedal cyclists, and motorcyclists - for the
        year to date.
        A snippet of this data is given in
        <xref ref="table-ntsa-daily-report"/>.
      </p>

