      <p>
        The second kind of record is the fatal accident report.
        There are two records.
        One at the crash-level which records one row per incident, giving the
        road, the place, the vehicles involved, and free-text on the accident
        (See <xref ref="table-ntsa-fatal-report"/> ).
        The other is at the victim-level which records one row per person.
        This has details on their name, gender, age, a code on the cause, and
        their status (driver, pedestrian, etc).
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice that the NTSA data is already multilevel in structure, even in
        this small snippet.
        The yearly data gives a summary of deaths, and so forth, for different
        victims (passengers, motorcyclists, etc).
        The crash-level table records one row per incident - the road, the
        place, the vehicles involved.
        The victim-level table records one row per person - their age, gender,
        and category.
        Different data can show different things.
        This is exactly the kind of structural decision discussed in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Notice that the NTSA data is already multilevel in structure, even in
        this small snippet.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The yearly data gives a summary of deaths over the year for
              different victims (passengers, motorcyclists, etc).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The crash-level table records one row per incident.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The victim-level table records one row per person.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        A single crash can involve multiple victims, meaning the tables are
        linked but not interchangeable: summarising at the crash level and
        summarising at the victim level will give you different counts and
        answer different questions.
        This is exactly the kind of structural decision we discussed in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
      </p>

