      <p>
        It was this data that Muguro et al.
        (2020), a team of researchers based at Gifu University in Japan and
        Dedan Kimathi University of Technology in Kenya, set out to analyse
        systematically for the first time.
      </p>

      <p>
        Muguro et al.
        (2020) found that there was relatively little systematic research on
        Kenya's road crashes despite the scale of the problem, and set out to
        analyse five years of NTSA data from 2015-2020.
        Their aim was to explore the occurrence of incidents on the roads,
        identify the leading causes, and identify where in Kenya the burden was
        greatest.
        Before any of that analysis could happen, they needed to understand the
        data itself: what it contained, how it was structured, and what
        variables it could answer questions about.
      </p>

      <p>
        A good place to start is Table 1 of their paper (reproduced in
        <xref ref="table-ntsa-crash-causes"/> ).
        It is a frequency table, showing how many incidents were recorded for
        each reported cause of crash, sorted from most to least common.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-ntsa-crash-causes">
