      <p>
        Immediately we can see that hit-and-runs were the most common cause of a
        crash in 2015-2020, by a wide margin, with 925 incidents.
        This is more than double the next cause!
        Excessive speeding, which arguably dominates public and media discussion
        of road danger in Kenya, appears seventh on the list with 120 incidents.
        This is over seven times fewer than hit-and-runs.
        We can see here that summarising this data has challenged a widely held
        assumption.
        That is what exploratory analysis does.
      </p>

      <p>
        Exploring the same data by time of day and by county produces
        <xref ref="fig-frequency-by-county-time"/> of Muguro et al.
        (2020), copied here.
        Two things stand out immediately.
        In the hourly distribution, the spike at 00:00 reflects crashes recorded
        with an unknown time rather than a genuine midnight peak; setting that
        aside, accidents concentrate between 16:00 and 21:00, consistent with
        evening rush hour.
        In the county distribution, Nairobi leads by a substantial margin,
        followed by Nakuru and Kiambu.
        This is partly a population effect since Nairobi is Kenya's most
        populous county so has the most cars, but the gap is wide enough to
        suggest that density and road conditions in the capital contribute
        something beyond population size alone.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-frequency-by-county-time">
        <caption>
          Hourly distribution of accidents with peak hours highlighted (top),
          and county distribution of accidents ranked by prevalence (bottom),
          Kenya 2015-2020.
          Note that the 00:00 spike reflects crashes with unknown recorded time.
          Reproduced from Muguro et al.
          (2020).
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_incidents.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two bar charts.
              The top chart shows the hourly distribution of road accidents
              across 24 hours, with a dashed box highlighting peak hours between
              approximately 16:00 and 21:00, and a spike at 00:00 representing
              crashes with unknown recorded time.
              The bottom chart shows accident counts by Kenyan county, ranked
              from highest to lowest prevalence, with Nairobi leading
              substantially, followed by Nakuru and Kiambu.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Beyond the structured fields in the NTSA records, each crash report
        contains a short free-text description of what happened.
        Muguro et al.
        (2020) explored this text too, using a technique called topic modelling
        to identify the recurring patterns of language across thousands of
        accident briefs.
        We do not go into "topic modelling" in detail here, but the basic idea
        is simple.
        Given a collection of short text descriptions, a topic model tries to
        find groups of words that tend to appear together, and treats each group
        as a theme or pattern (or "topic") that recurs across the documents.
        The analyst chooses how many topics to look for, and then inspects the
        results to see whether the groupings make sense.
      </p>

      <p>
        The results from the paper are shown in the word clouds in
        <xref ref="fig-word-clouds-lda"/> for models with three, four, five, and
        six topics.
        In a three-topic model, the algorithm is asked to sort all the accident
        descriptions into three groups; in a four-topic model, four groups; and
        so on.
        Even without knowing anything about the method, the clusters are
        readable: one topic is dominated by <q>hit</q>, <q>run</q>, and
        <q>collision</q>; another by <q>knocked</q>, <q>victim</q>, and
        <q>down</q>; a third by <q>lost</q>, <q>control</q>, and <q>road</q>.
        The four-topic model captures these categories most cleanly with hit and
        run, head-on collision, loss of control, and pedestrian knockdown.
        Meanwhile, the six-topic model begins to fragment into overlapping
        categories.
        Choosing how many topics to extract is itself an exploratory decision,
        guided by whether the resulting clusters are interpretable and distinct.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-word-clouds-lda">
        <caption>
          Word clouds from topic modelling of NTSA accident brief descriptions,
          for three-, four-, five-, and six-topic models.
          Word size reflects frequency within each topic.
          Reproduced from Muguro et al.
          (2020), <em>Cogent Engineering</em>, 7(1), 1797981, under CC BY 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_wordclouds.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Four panels showing word clouds from topic models fitted to NTSA
              accident brief text.
              Panel (a) shows a three-topic model with clusters around
              collision/hit/run, vehicle/knocked/victim/down, and
              road/vehicle/control/lost.
              Panel (b) shows a four-topic model separating these into hit/run,
              knocked/victim/down, road/vehicle/control/lost, and
              head/collision.
              Panels (c) and (d) show five- and six-topic models where
              categories begin to fragment and overlap.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Another exploratory approach, applied to victims rather than causes,
        produced a Table replicated in <xref ref="table-ntsa-monthly-stats"/>.
        Muguro et al.
        (2020) grouped the monthly records by road user category and computed
        summaries for each - average monthly fatalities and injuries, standard
        deviation, minimum, and maximum.
        The numbers make the burden on vulnerable road users concrete.
      </p>

