      <p>
        The passenger injury average of 490 per month is nearly ten times the
        pedestrian injury average of 103, and exceeds any other category.
        This summary led the authors to scale that column into a boxplot in
        <xref ref="fig-muguro-boxplot"/> to make the other categories visible.
        The motorcyclist category shows the widest range in injuries of any
        group (39 to 217 per month), consistent with the rapid growth of
        boda-boda transport over the period and the high variability in
        enforcement and road conditions across counties.
        Both patterns are visible in the exploratory table before any deeper
        descriptive analysis or single model is fitted.
      </p>

      <p>
        Muguro et al (2020) makes those same patterns visible in a boxplot of
        monthly fatalities and injuries (See <xref ref="fig-muguro-boxplot"/> ),
        one box per road user category, show not just the averages but the
        spread and the outliers.
        Pedestrians dominate fatalities.
        The passenger injury box is so large it had to be scaled down by a
        factor of four.
        Motorcyclists show wide variation in both panels.
        These are not sophisticated visualisations.
        They are the kinds of plots you produce in the first hour of exploring a
        new dataset.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-muguro-boxplot">
        <caption>
          Monthly fatalities and injuries by road user category in Kenya,
          2015-2020.
          Passenger injuries (marked with asterisk) have been scaled by 0.25 to
          fit within the range of other categories.
          From Muguro et al.
          (2020)
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_boxplots.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two boxplots showing monthly fatalities (top) and injuries
              (bottom) for six road user categories: Pedestrians, Passengers,
              Drivers, Pillion Passengers, Cyclists, and Motor-Cyclists.
              Pedestrians and Passengers dominate fatalities.
              Passenger injuries are scaled by 0.25.
              Motorcyclists and Pillion Passengers show wide variation in both
              panels.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        What Muguro et al.
        (2020) found was clear.
        Nairobi, Nakuru, Kiambu, Machakos, and Kakamega accounted for the
        largest share of fatal crashes, consistent with high population density
        and increased motorisation.
        Pedestrians, pillion passengers, and motorcyclists were dying at
        dramatically increasing rates: injuries to this group of vulnerable road
        users had increased by more than 250 percent over the five-year period.
        Overall, fatalities on Kenyan roads increased by 26 percent and injuries
        by 46.5 percent between January 2015 and January 2020.
      </p>

      <p>
        What we have looked at from their research is just their exploratory
        phase.
        The patterns that emerged included rising fatalities, a disproportionate
        burden on boda-boda riders and pedestrians, and hit-and-run as the
        leading recorded cause.
        These patterns directly informed the paper's recommendations: targeted
        enforcement against hit-and-run behaviour, infrastructure and policy
        attention to the counties where deaths concentrate, and urgent action to
        protect vulnerable road users whose injury rates had tripled over five
        years.
        The authors also flagged that road crashes were estimated to cost Kenya
        between 3 and 5 percent of GDP annually — a figure that turns a table of
        counts into an argument for investment.
        The NTSA had been collecting the raw material for years.
        Here is just an example of how exploring it systematically can make
        those arguments possible.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ntsa-exploration">
        <title>Exploration is not preliminary. It is often the work.</title>

        <p>
          The NTSA data existed before Muguro et al.
          (2020) asked these questions.
          The concentration of deaths in five counties, the vulnerability of
          boda-boda riders, the sharp upward trend in fatalities, and so forth
          was sitting in the records, waiting to be explored.
          This chapter is about the tools and habits of mind that let you do
          that: to take a dataset you have never seen before, and find out what
          it is actually telling you.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The dataset underlying this kind of analysis is also publicly available.
        The Humanitarian Data Exchange (HDX) hosts a Kenya Road Accidents
        database - a real extract of NTSA crash records, covering April to June
        2016, with fields including county, road, time of crash, vehicle type,
        and road user category.
        This is available
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">here</url>.
        It is the same kind of data Muguro et al.
        worked with, and it is the dataset we will use for some of the exercises
        in this chapter.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Muguro, J.K., Sasaki, M., Matsushita, K.
        and Njeri, W.
        (2020).
        <q>Trend analysis and fatality causes in Kenyan 
    roads: A review of road traffic accident data between 2015 and 
    2020.</q>
        <em>Cogent Engineering</em>, 7(1), 1797981.
        <url href="https://doi.org/10.1080/23311916.2020.1797981">doi.org/10.1080/23311916.2020.1797981</url>.
        Licensed under CC BY 4.0.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dataset: Kenya Road Accidents database, openAFRICA / Humanitarian Data
        Exchange.
        Available at:
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
