  <section xml:id="sec-ntsa-r-exploration">
    <title>Exploring the Kenya Road Accidents Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Kenya Road Accidents database used by Muguro et al.
        is publicly available from the Humanitarian Data Exchange.
        You can download it directly from
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>.
        The dataset covers fatal accident reports from April to June 2016, with
        378 rows and 13 columns.
        Save the file somewhere you can find it, then load it into R.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before writing code to explore a dataset, it helps to understand what
        kind of data you are working with.
        Data generally falls into two broad types: <term>categorical data</term>
        (labels, names, or groups, like county or victim category) and
        <term>numerical data</term> (counts, measurements, or continuous
        numbers, like age or the time of a crash).
        Each type calls for different tools.
        We will meet both in this dataset.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you have not already installed the packages we will use in this
        section, run the following once:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        install.packages("rpivotTable")
        install.packages("readxl")
        install.packages("dplyr")
        install.packages("ggplot2")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Then load the packages and read in the data.
        You can either specify the file path directly, or use the
        <em>Import Dataset</em> button in the Environment panel in RStudio
        (click <em>From Excel...</em> ) to navigate to the file.
      </p>
      <p>
                TODO: insert image
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(rpivotTable)
        library(readxl)
        library(dplyr)
        library(ggplot2)

        kenya_accidents &lt;- read_excel("kenya-accidents-database.xlsx")
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-first-look">
      <title>A first look at the data</title>

      <p>
        Before doing anything else, it is worth getting a feel for the data: how
        many rows and columns, what the columns are called, and what a few rows
        look like.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # first few rows
        head(kenya_accidents)

        # dimensions: rows and columns
        dim(kenya_accidents)
        #&gt; [1] 378  13

        # column names and types
        glimpse(kenya_accidents)
        #&gt; Rows: 378
        #&gt; Columns: 13
        #&gt; $ `TIME 24 HOURS`          &lt;chr&gt; "630", "830", "1330", ...
        #&gt; $ `BASE/SUB BASE`          &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI", "VOI", ...
        #&gt; $ COUNTY                   &lt;chr&gt; "MAKUENI", "TAITA TAVETA", ...
        #&gt; $ ROAD                     &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI-ITHOKWE", ...
        #&gt; $ PLACE                    &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI SCHOOL", "IKANGA", ...
        #&gt; $ `BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS` &lt;chr&gt; "HEAD ON COLLISION", ...
        #&gt; $ GENDER                   &lt;chr&gt; "M", "M", "M", ...
        #&gt; $ AGE                      &lt;chr&gt; "26", "28", "A &amp; J", ...
        #&gt; $ `CAUSE CODE`             &lt;dbl&gt; 7, 25, 98, 29, ...
        #&gt; $ VICTIM                   &lt;chr&gt; "M/CYCLIST", "M/CYCLIST", ...
        #&gt; $ NO.                      &lt;dbl&gt; 1, 1, 1, 1, ...
        #&gt; $ `Date DD/MM/YYYY`        &lt;dttm&gt; 2016-06-25, 2016-06-25, ...
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        A few things are immediately apparent.
        The <c>AGE</c> column has values like <c>"A &amp; J"</c>
        (presumably Adult and Juvenile) mixed in with numeric ages. As a result,
        R has treated the entire column as text rather than numbers.
        The <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column is also text, and contains some
        ambiguous values.
        There is also a mysterious 13th column with a stray note in it.
        All of this is normal when working with real data, and spotting these
        issues early is exactly what exploratory analysis is for.
        We will come back to data cleaning in
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>; for now, we work with what we have.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-pivot">
      <title>Summarising data with a pivot table</title>

      <p>
        When dealing with hundreds or thousands of rows of raw information, it
        is impossible to spot patterns by eye.
        A <term>pivot table</term> is one of the most useful tools for
        summarising data quickly.
        It performs three core actions: it <em>splits</em> your data by a
        categorical variable, <em>applies</em> a calculation to each group (such
        as a count, a sum, or an average), and <em>combines</em> the results
        into a compact summary.
        The <c>rpivotTable</c> package brings this directly into R as an
        interactive tool: drag variables into rows and columns and the table
        updates instantly.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Try dragging <c>COUNTY</c> into the row area.
        You will immediately see that Nairobi has 72 incidents in the dataset,
        almost one for every day of the three-month period covered.
        Move <c>COUNTY</c> to the top and <c>Date</c> to the left, and you get a
        breakdown of accidents by county by date: Kiambu, Nakuru, and Nairobi
        show incidents on almost every day.
        Change the display from counts to a heatmap and the concentration in
        those counties becomes even more visible.
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: add image
      </p>

      <p>
        Now try adding <c>VICTIM</c> alongside <c>COUNTY</c>.
        In Nairobi, most victims are pedestrians.
        In Makueni, the picture is quite different. There are only two pedestrians,
        but 8 motorcyclists, 6 passengers, and 7 drivers.
        The county-level pattern from Muguro et al. (2020)
        starts to emerge even in this small three-month slice of data.
      </p>

      <p>
        Pivot tables are also useful for spotting data quality issues: if you
        put <c>COUNTY</c> in rows and <c>VICTIM</c> in columns, you will see
        both <c>M/CYCLIST</c> and <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c> appear as separate
        categories, almost certainly the same category entered inconsistently.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-categorical">
      <title>Visualising data</title>

      <p>
        Categorical data tells you which group an observation belongs to.
        The primary tool for visualising it is a <term>bar chart</term>, where
        the length of each bar represents how many observations fall into each
        category.
        This makes it easy to compare groups at a glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        For the categorical columns in our data, converting them to factors
        before calling <c>summary()</c> gives us counts for each level rather
        than just a description of the column type:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents$COUNTY &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents$COUNTY)
        kenya_accidents$GENDER &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents$GENDER)
        kenya_accidents$VICTIM &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents$VICTIM)

        summary(kenya_accidents$COUNTY)
        summary(kenya_accidents$COUNTY)
        #&gt;           BARINGO           BOMET         BUNGOMA           BUSIA ELGEYO MARAKWET 
        #&gt;                 6               4              10               3               2 
        #&gt;              EMBU         GARISSA        HOMA BAY         KAJIADO        KAKAMEGA 
        #&gt;                10               3              14              12              10 
        #&gt;            KERCHO         KERICHO          KIAMBU          KILIFI       KIRINYAGA 
        #&gt;                 1               5              31              18               3 
        #&gt;             KISII          KISUMU           KITUI           KWALE        LAIKIPIA 
        #&gt;                12               7               2               4               2 
        #&gt;          MACHAKOS         MAKUENI          MAKURU        MARAKWET            MERU 
        #&gt;                 9              26               1               1               5 
        #&gt;            MIGORI         MOMBASA        MURANG'A         MURANGA          MWINGI 
        #&gt;                 5              13               3               3               3 
        #&gt;           NAIROBI          NAKURU           NANDI           NAROK       NYAHURURU 
        #&gt;                71              32               1               9               1 
        #&gt;           NYAMIRA       NYANDARUA           NYERI           SIAYA    TAITA TAVETA 
        #&gt;                 1               1               8               4               4 
        #&gt;       TRANS NZOIA     UASIN GISHU          VIHIGA            NA's 
        #&gt;                 5               7               4               2 

        summary(kenya_accidents_database$ROAD)
        #&gt;         NAIROBI MOMBASA          MOMBASA NAIROBI       THIKA SUPERHIGHWAY 
        #&gt;                      14                       10                        8 
        #&gt;         MOMBASA-NAIROBI                  MOMBASA                 KANGUNDO 
        #&gt;                       7                        6                        5 
        #&gt;          NAKURU ELDORET           NAKURU NAIROBI           ELDORET NAKURU 
        #&gt;                       5                        5                        4
        #&gt;           ... and so forth!

        summary(kenya_accidents$GENDER)
        #&gt;   2F &amp; M  2M &amp; 2F  2M &amp; 3F    2M, F 4F &amp; INF        F        M    M &amp; F 
        #&gt;        1        1        1        1        1       51      321        1 
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Nairobi has the highest frequency of incidents by a substantial margin,
        followed by Nakuru and Kiambu — matching the county concentration Muguro
        et al.(2020) found across the full five years.
        When looking at roads, the Nairobi-Mombasa route comes up the most frequently
        as the road with the most incidents.
        Male victims outnumber female victims by more than six to one.
        The entries like <c>"M &amp; F"</c> and <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c> are crashes
        with multiple victims of different genders recorded in a single row each.
        This is another data quality issue that exploration surfaces.
      </p>

      <p>
        Summaries tell us the counts; plots make the distribution visible.
        A bar chart of county gives an immediate sense of where incidents
        concentrate, and using <c>coord_flip()</c> rotates the axis labels to
        make the county names readable:
      </p>
      <p>
        Barcharts can be created by using <c>barchart</c> function, or by
        <c>ggplot2</c> which is discussed in more detail in the Visualising
        Multidimensionality chapter.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # one approach
        barchart(kenya_accidents$COUNTY)
        </code>
      </program>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents, aes(x = COUNTY)) +
          geom_bar() +
          coord_flip() +
          labs(
            title = "Fatal road accidents by county, Kenya 2016",
            x = "County",
            y = "Number of incidents"
          )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        TODO: put in plots.
      </p>

      <p>
        Similarly for victim category:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(kenya_accidents, aes(x = VICTIM)) +
          geom_bar() +
          coord_flip() +
          labs(
            title = "Fatal road accidents by victim category, Kenya 2016",
            x = "Victim category",
            y = "Number of incidents"
          )
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        TODO: put in plots.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are not polished publication-ready figures.
        They are exploratory plots — the kind you produce quickly to get a feel
        for what the data contains before deciding what to look at more
        carefully.
        We go into more powerful and refined visualisation in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-numerical">
      <title>Visualising numerical data</title>

      <p>
        Numerical data represents quantifiable measurements.
        When visualising numbers, the goal shifts from counting groups to
        understanding the <em>spread</em> and <em>distribution</em> of values.
        The main tools are histograms, which show how values are distributed
        across their full range, and boxplots, which efficiently summarise the
        spread and highlight outliers.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>AGE</c> column needs to be converted to numeric first. The
        non-numeric entries like <c>"A &amp; J"</c> will become <c>NA</c>, which
        is fine for now:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents$AGE &lt;- as.numeric(kenya_accidents$AGE)
        summary(kenya_accidents$AGE)
        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.    NA's 
        #&gt;    1.50   23.00   30.00   31.53   40.00   90.00     193
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Ages range from 1.5 to 90 years, with a median of 30.
        Note that 193 of the 378 rows have no usable age recorded.
        A histogram shows the full distribution:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        hist(kenya_accidents$AGE,
             main = "Age of road accident victims, Kenya 2016",
             xlab = "Age (years)",
             breaks = 20)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        TODO: give image of histogram. 
      </p>

      <p>
        There are notable spikes at ages 30, 35, 40, 50, 60, and 70 — multiples
        of five and ten — which is a classic sign of age rounding in the
        original records.
        People were recording estimated rather than exact ages.
        A boxplot makes the spread and any outliers visible more directly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        boxplot(kenya_accidents$AGE,
                main = "Age of road accident victims, Kenya 2016",
                ylab = "Age (years)")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        TODO: give image of boxplot.
      </p>

      <p>
        The 90-year-old appears as an isolated point above the main
        distribution.
        The box itself covers the middle 50% of the data. It sits roughly
        between 23 and 40 years old, with a median around 30.
        The highest concentration of incidents occurs for people approximately
        20 to 40 years old: working-age adults. Are these the
        boda-boda riders and pedestrians we saw dominating the victim
        categories? We can explore that too.
      </p>

      <p>
        Scatter plots are the standard tool for examining the relationship
        between two numerical variables.
        The Kenya accidents data has few pairs of numerical variables to compare
        directly, but in <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/> we
        will use scatter plots extensively on richer datasets.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
