    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-bill-length">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Before computing anything, predict: do you expect
          <c>bill_length_mm</c> to look like a single hump, or two, once
          plotted?
          Now load the data, plot a histogram of <c>bill_length_mm</c>, and
          check.
          If you see more than one hump, try colouring or splitting by
          <c>species</c> and see whether that explains it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-lake-sex">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Build a pivot table of <c>lake</c> against <c>sex</c>.
          Does the balance of males and females look different from lake to
          lake?
          Say which comparison (row proportions or column proportions) you used
          to answer that, and why you chose it.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-write-up">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Pick any one numeric variable in the dataset other than
          <c>body_mass_g</c> or <c>bill_length_mm</c>.
          Look at it, however you like: a histogram, a table of rounded values,
          sorting it and scanning by eye.
          Write two or three sentences describing what you notice and one
          question it raises that you cannot yet answer.
          There is no single correct answer here; the point is practising the
          habit of looking before summarising.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>